---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// V-709 — Sentry integration guide. Pitched at customers who already
// run Sentry for their own services and want errors from their
// Driftstack-driven browser sessions to land in the same project.
// Sister page to /docs/cost-monitoring (alert configuration) and
// /docs/webhooks (event consumption).
---

<BaseLayout title="Sentry integration — Driftstack" description="Forward errors from Driftstack browser sessions into your Sentry project: webhook fan-out, source map propagation, release tagging.">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <h1>Sentry integration</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      Driftstack runs a fleet of browser sessions on your behalf;
      this guide shows how to route the unhandled errors + script
      failures from those sessions into your existing Sentry
      project. The integration is webhook-driven — no SDK to install
      on Driftstack's side.
    </p>

    <h2>The integration shape</h2>
    <p>
      You give us a Sentry <strong>store endpoint URL</strong> and a
      DSN public key. We hash + forward each failed session's stack
      trace + breadcrumb trail to that endpoint. From your side it
      looks like any other Sentry-instrumented service — same project,
      same release filters, same alerts.
    </p>

    <h2>Set it up</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Create a Sentry project (or reuse an existing one).</strong>
        Settings → Projects → Create Project; pick "Browser
        JavaScript" as the platform so the stack-trace handling
        matches.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Copy the DSN.</strong> Format:
        <code>https://&lt;public_key&gt;@sentry.io/&lt;project_id&gt;</code>.
        Only the public key half is sent server-side; treat it as a
        write-only credential.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Add it to Driftstack.</strong> In the dashboard,
        Settings → Integrations → Sentry. Paste the DSN + click Save.
        We send a probe event on save so you can verify in Sentry's
        Issues view that we wired the right project.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What gets forwarded</h2>
    <p>
      Two event types land in Sentry:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>session.failed</code> with a captured stack trace.
        The browser's <code>error</code> event handler fires inside
        the sandbox; we capture the message + stack + the last 50
        navigation / network / console breadcrumbs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>script.error</code> when a customer-supplied script
        body throws. The Driftstack session id is set as the Sentry
        <code>tags.session_id</code> so you can drill back to the
        live-view replay.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Source maps</h2>
    <p>
      If your script body is bundled and minified, attach a source
      map via the upload-source-map field in <code>sessions.start</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`await client.sessions.start({
  target_url: 'https://example.com',
  script: BUNDLED_SCRIPT,
  source_map_url: 'https://your-cdn.example/bundle.js.map',
});`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      We pass the URL through to Sentry's <code>store</code> endpoint
      so Sentry's symbolicator can resolve frames against your map.
      Self-hosted Sentry deployments work too — set the
      <code>SENTRY_BASE_URL</code> in the integration settings.
    </p>

    <h2>Release tagging</h2>
    <p>
      Pass a <code>release</code> tag on <code>sessions.start</code> so
      Sentry's "Resolved in release" tracking and regression filters
      work:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`await client.sessions.start({
  target_url: 'https://example.com',
  script: BUNDLED_SCRIPT,
  release: 'my-app@2.3.1',
});`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Without it, all events land under a synthetic
      <code>driftstack-session</code> release — usable but coarse.
    </p>

    <h2>Sampling + rate-limiting</h2>
    <p>
      The integration honours Sentry's per-project quota: if Sentry
      returns a 429, we back off with exponential delay (60s, 120s,
      240s, then capped at 5m). The session itself is not affected —
      the error fan-out is best-effort.
    </p>
    <p>
      For high-volume scrapers, configure
      <code>sentry_sample_rate</code> in the integration settings
      (0.0 – 1.0). Recommended: 1.0 in staging, 0.1 in production
      until you've sized the Sentry quota.
    </p>

    <h2>What does NOT get forwarded</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Successful sessions.</strong> We only forward on the
        terminal <code>failed</code> path; a flaky-but-eventually-
        successful session doesn't fire.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Test sessions.</strong> Sessions started from a
        <code>ds_test_</code> API key are excluded by default to keep
        your Sentry quota clean during development. Override with
        <code>sentry_forward_test_sessions=true</code> in the
        integration settings if you want them included.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Network errors from the page under test.</strong>
        Those are part of the page's own surface — instrument Sentry
        inside your <code>target_url</code> if you care.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Audit + debugging</h2>
    <p>
      Every Sentry POST we make is logged to <strong>Audit log</strong>
      with a one-line summary (session id + Sentry event id once
      Sentry responds 200). If you see no events arriving, check:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Audit log entries — confirm we attempted to send.</li>
      <li>Sentry's <strong>Stats</strong> view — confirm Sentry
        received + accepted (vs dropped by quota / sample rate).</li>
      <li>Integration health row in Settings → Integrations — we mark
        the integration <em>degraded</em> after 3 consecutive non-2xx
        responses.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Disabling the integration</h2>
    <p>
      Settings → Integrations → Sentry → Disable. The DSN is
      preserved (you can re-enable later); deletion clears it
      permanently. Pending events in the retry queue are dropped on
      disable.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/docs/webhooks">Webhooks</a> — the general-purpose
        event-fan-out surface; Sentry is one preset destination.</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/audit-log">Audit log</a> — where the
        forwarder's actions land.</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/error-codes">Error codes</a> — the structured
        codes that prefix Sentry event titles.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Need help?</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="mailto:integrations@driftstack.dev">integrations@driftstack.dev</a>.
      Include your Sentry organisation slug + the integration health
      row from the dashboard.
    </p>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
